  "articles": [
    {
      "title": "About syllabics",
      "body": "<p>Canadian syllabics were created in 1840 by James Evans, a missionary, working with Cree speakers in what is now Manitoba. The system spread quickly because it is so regular, and was adapted for Ojibwe, Cree, Naskapi, Carrier, Blackfoot and, from the 1850s, Inuktitut.</p>\n<p>Today Inuktitut syllabics are an official script of Nunavut and are used in Nunavik (northern Quebec). They appear on signs, in government documents and newspapers, and on phones and computers. Other Inuit regions write Inuktut in the Latin alphabet.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "How the shapes work",
      "body": "<p>Each consonant has one shape. Its direction gives the vowel:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><td lang=\"iu\">ᐱ ᐳ ᐸ</td><td>pi, pu, pa: the same shape pointing up, right and left</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"iu\">ᑎ ᑐ ᑕ</td><td>ti, tu, ta</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"iu\">ᓂ ᓄ ᓇ</td><td>ni, nu, na</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>A small raised version of the a-form is the consonant with no vowel: <span lang=\"iu\">ᑉ</span> p, <span lang=\"iu\">ᑦ</span> t, <span lang=\"iu\">ᓐ</span> n. So <span lang=\"iu\">ᐃᓄᒃ</span> is i + nu + k: inuk, a person.</p>\n<p>Inuktitut has only three vowels (i, u, a), which is why there are three directions. Syllabics for Cree also use an e-direction.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Long vowels",
      "body": "<p>A dot over a syllable makes its vowel long: <span lang=\"iu\">ᒥ</span> mi, <span lang=\"iu\">ᒦ</span> mii. In the word for thank you, <span lang=\"iu\">ᖁᔭᓐᓇᒦᒃ</span> (qujannamiik), the dot on <span lang=\"iu\">ᒦ</span> marks the long ii. Some writers leave the dots out, and some write the vowel twice instead (<span lang=\"iu\">ᒥᐃ</span>).</p>"
    }
  ]
}
